-- ai-jobs-uk-bank-of-england-hiring-freeze-20261106 (967 words; primary keyword in title, first paragraph, subheading)
INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-jobs-uk-bank-of-england-hiring-freeze-20261106', 'global', 'United Kingdom',
'AI Jobs in the UK: Bank of England Says AI Is Freezing Hiring, Not Firing Workers',
'',
'AI jobs in the UK face a quiet hiring freeze rather than mass layoffs, the Bank of England November Monetary Policy Report says. Firms are leaving vacancies unfilled as AI takes on routine work, while almost no workers are being dismissed because of it.',
'',
$$<p>AI jobs in the UK face a quiet hiring freeze rather than mass layoffs, the Bank of England said in its November Monetary Policy Report, published on 5 November 2026. Firms responding to the Bank's Decision Maker Panel, a monthly survey of company finance chiefs, reported close to zero impact from artificial intelligence on employment so far. But the same survey found AI adoption is reducing the likelihood of firms hiring or replacing workers, while not materially increasing the likelihood of firing them. The result is a labour market where roles disappear through vacancies left unfilled, rather than through redundancy notices.</p>
<p>That distinction matters for anyone looking for work. When a worker leaves and the post is not filled, the number of openings for jobseekers and job-switchers falls, yet unemployment stays low because nobody has been dismissed. ING put it plainly: "AI is not firing people; it is quietly not hiring them." The Bank's own Agents, its regional contacts with businesses across the country, report the same pattern. The hiring slowdown is real, but it works through empty chairs rather than exit interviews, with no sign yet of the mass displacement some forecasts warned about.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>What the Bank of England reported</th></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>November Monetary Policy Report, published 5 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>AI impact on jobs so far</td><td>Close to zero in aggregate, Decision Maker Panel survey</td></tr><tr><td>Effect on hiring</td><td>AI reducing likelihood of hiring or replacing staff; no material rise in firing</td></tr><tr><td>Businesses using AI</td><td>Three in ten UK businesses (Pound Sterling Live)</td></tr><tr><td>Headcount among AI users</td><td>6% report a reduction, fewer than 1% an increase (firms with 10 or more staff)</td></tr><tr><td>Productivity gain so far</td><td>About 0.5% over three years</td></tr><tr><td>Productivity gain expected</td><td>About 0.9 percentage points a year over the next three years</td></tr><tr><td>UK unemployment</td><td>4.7% in the three months to August 2026; Bank projects a peak of 5.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Next data check</td><td>ONS quarterly productivity estimates, 17 November 2026</td></tr></table>
<h2>What the Bank found on AI jobs in the UK</h2>
<p>Three in ten UK businesses now use AI, Pound Sterling Live reports from the Bank's research. Among AI users with ten or more staff, 6% said the technology had reduced their headcount, against fewer than 1% who said it had increased it. Administrative and clerical roles are the most affected, routine office work that AI tools handle most readily. The pattern is uneven: firms using AI at scale report they can automate routine administrative work and speed up knowledge tasks, while smaller users see little change. For jobseekers, the competition is not a machine taking a specific job, but a vacancy that never appears.</p>
<p>The Bank also set out what AI is doing to productivity. Firms in the Decision Maker Panel survey estimated that AI has raised their productivity by around 0.5% over the past three years, which the Bank said points to AI adding about 0.1 to 0.2 percentage points a year to productivity growth since early 2024. Looking ahead, the same firms expect AI to lift productivity growth by about 0.9 percentage points a year over the next three years, split roughly equally between slower employment growth and faster output growth. In other words, about half of the expected gain would come from employing fewer people than would otherwise be the case. The Bank called AI a structural force, likely to last, unlike the cyclical factors behind most of the recent productivity strength.</p>
<h2>Why the Bank says most of the squeeze is not AI</h2>
<p>The Bank was careful to say that AI explains only a modest part of the recent jobs squeeze. Most of the strength in productivity growth since early 2024 looks cyclical: firms hoarded labour when hiring was difficult in 2022 and 2023, then hired fewer workers as output picked up. That cannot run for ever, and the Bank's Agents report some firms have now reached a lower bound for headcount. Higher labour costs have also played a part. Increases in the National Living Wage and employer National Insurance contributions have cut lower-paid jobs in retail and hospitality, sectors with less exposure to AI. Unemployment stood at 4.7% in the three months to August, and the Bank projects a peak of 5.1%, with no widespread labour market shakeout expected. Berenberg said predictions of imminent mass unemployment look far-fetched, and argued AI will almost certainly create new jobs as well, as past waves of technology have done.</p>
<h2>What to do next if you are job hunting</h2>
<p>If you are job hunting in the UK, the practical lesson is to aim where hiring is still happening. Roles pairing AI tools with human judgement, such as data analysis and skilled trades, are less exposed than routine administrative posts. Workers in administrative and clerical roles should treat AI skills as a CV asset rather than a threat: short courses in using AI tools for drafting, spreadsheets and scheduling are widely available free of charge. The pattern is not confined to Britain. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-replacing-jobs-korea-gen-z-factory-floors-20261106">AI is already reshaping hiring in South Korea</a>, where young graduates are turning to factory work, and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-uae-workers-fear-job-losses-20261106">workers in the UAE fear AI job losses</a> in a separate survey. Check the employer's own careers page and apply through official channels only, and keep an eye on <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/uk-job-adverts-salary-law-burnham-20261106">UK job adverts, which may soon have to show salary ranges</a>.</p>
<p>The Bank's decision on interest rates sits alongside the jobs picture. At the same meeting, the Monetary Policy Committee voted 8-1 to raise Bank Rate to 4.00%, the first increase in more than three years. Pound Sterling Live noted that private non-financial companies held £576.4bn in sterling bank deposits at the end of August, the highest since January 2023, which suggests firms have the cash to hire if they choose. A rate cut would mainly lower borrowing costs for firms that need credit, and would do little to change technology-driven recruitment decisions. The hiring freeze, in other words, looks like a choice about how firms organise work, not a shortage of money.</p>
<p>The next test of the Bank's reading comes on 17 November 2026, when the Office for National Statistics publishes its new quarterly productivity estimates. If output per hour keeps rising, the hiring slowdown will show up in official data rather than in surveys alone. The Bank said it will keep monitoring productivity and the labour market closely. For anyone chasing AI jobs in the UK, the number to watch is not the unemployment rate alone, but the vacancy count: as long as firms keep leaving posts unfilled, the jobs market will feel tight even while few people lose their jobs.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Bank of England at https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-report/2026/november-2026 on the Decision Maker Panel findings, the productivity figures and the 17 November ONS date; Pound Sterling Live at https://www.poundsterlinglive.com/economics/23846-ai-jobs-uk-hiring-slowdown-bank-of-england-productivity on the hiring freeze, the business adoption figures and the ING and Berenberg assessments.</small></p>
$$,
'',
